"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[4673],{704673:e=>{e.exports='# Feature Engineering with Declarative Feature Pipelines\n\nThis guide covers feature engineering using the Databricks Feature Engineering API. Declarative feature pipelines provide a unified way to define, compute, and manage features.\n\n> **Note:** If there is any contradiction between the user\'s explicit preferences or instructions and the guidance in this skill, always defer to the user\'s preferences.\n\n## Overview\n\nA declarative feature is defined as a structured object:\n\n```\nFeature {\n  name: catalog.schema.feature_name     # Unity Catalog object\n  source: DeltaTableSource | DataFrameSource | KafkaSource  # Input data\n  function: Aggregation | TableLookup | UDF | UDAF          # How to compute\n  entity_columns: [...]                 # Aggregation granularity\n  timeseries_column: ...                # Timestamp for windowing\n  time_window: ContinuousWindow | TumblingWindow | SlidingWindow\n}\n```\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nInstall the required package:\n\n```python\n%pip install databricks-feature-engineering\n```\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Data to Determine Feature Strategy\n\nBefore defining features, analyze the data during EDA to inform your approach:\n\n- **Identify entity types**: What are the natural grouping keys? (e.g., customer_id, merchant_id, store_id)\n- **Check for timestamps**: Does the data have a temporal dimension? What is the event frequency?\n- **Identify lookup relationships**: Are there reference tables that provide static attributes per entity?\n- **Determine time window granularity** based on transaction frequency:\n  - High-frequency data (multiple events per hour) → include sub-daily windows (1h, 3h, 6h)\n  - Daily-frequency data → use daily and weekly windows (1d, 7d, 14d, 30d)\n  - Sparse data (weekly or less) → use longer windows (7d, 30d, 90d)\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ndf = spark.read.table("catalog.schema.transactions")\n\n# Transaction frequency per entity per day\nentity_daily = df.groupBy(\n    "customer_id", F.to_date("transaction_time").alias("date")\n).count().groupBy("customer_id").agg(\n    F.avg("count").alias("avg_txn_per_day"),\n    F.max("count").alias("max_txn_per_day")\n)\ndisplay(entity_daily.orderBy(F.desc("avg_txn_per_day")).limit(20))\n```\n\n## Step 2: Define Data Sources\n\nCreate a separate `DeltaTableSource` for each entity granularity. Different entities aggregate at different levels and require separate source objects.\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import DeltaTableSource\n\n# Customer-level features (from transactional data)\ncustomer_source = DeltaTableSource(\n    catalog_name="catalog",\n    schema_name="schema",\n    table_name="transactions",\n    entity_columns=["customer_id"],\n    timeseries_column="transaction_time"\n)\n\n# Merchant-level features (same table, different entity granularity)\nmerchant_source = DeltaTableSource(\n    catalog_name="catalog",\n    schema_name="schema",\n    table_name="transactions",\n    entity_columns=["merchant_id"],\n    timeseries_column="transaction_time"\n)\n```\n\nKey rules:\n\n- `entity_columns` defines the aggregation granularity (GROUP BY columns)\n- `timeseries_column` must be `TimestampType` or `DateType`\n- If you need customer-merchant pair features, create a third source with `entity_columns=["customer_id", "merchant_id"]`\n- Entity and timeseries column names must match between training data and source tables\n\n## Step 3: Define Features\n\nUse `FeatureEngineeringClient.create_feature()` to define features declaratively.\n\n### Available Aggregation Functions\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import (\n    Sum, Avg, Count, Min, Max,\n    StddevPop, StddevSamp, VarPop, VarSamp,\n    ApproxCountDistinct, ApproxPercentile,\n    First, Last\n)\n```\n\n**IMPORTANT:** There is no `CountDistinct` function. Use `ApproxCountDistinct` for distinct counts. `ApproxCountDistinct(relativeSD=0.05)` accepts an optional relative standard deviation parameter.\n\n### Available Time Windows\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import (\n    ContinuousWindow,   # Sliding lookback from evaluation time (most common)\n    TumblingWindow,      # Fixed non-overlapping windows\n    SlidingWindow        # Overlapping windows with slide interval\n)\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n```\n\n- **ContinuousWindow**: Best for training/scoring — computes point-in-time correct aggregates looking back from each row\'s timestamp. Supports `offset` (must be <= 0) to exclude recent unstable data.\n- **TumblingWindow**: Fixed, non-overlapping periods. Each event belongs to exactly one window.\n- **SlidingWindow**: Overlapping windows advancing by `slide_duration`. Events can belong to multiple windows.\n\n### Example: Time-Windowed Aggregation Features\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering import FeatureEngineeringClient\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import (\n    DeltaTableSource, Sum, Avg, Count, ApproxCountDistinct,\n    ContinuousWindow\n)\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nfe = FeatureEngineeringClient()\n\nCATALOG = "catalog"\nSCHEMA = "schema"\n\ncustomer_source = DeltaTableSource(\n    catalog_name=CATALOG,\n    schema_name=SCHEMA,\n    table_name="transactions",\n    entity_columns=["customer_id"],\n    timeseries_column="transaction_time"\n)\n\ntime_windows = [\n    timedelta(hours=1),\n    timedelta(hours=6),\n    timedelta(days=1),\n    timedelta(days=7),\n    timedelta(days=30),\n]\n\nfeatures = []\nfor window in time_windows:\n    features.extend([\n        fe.create_feature(\n            catalog_name=CATALOG,\n            schema_name=SCHEMA,\n            source=customer_source,\n            inputs=["amount"],\n            function=Sum(),\n            time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=window)\n        ),\n        fe.create_feature(\n            catalog_name=CATALOG,\n            schema_name=SCHEMA,\n            source=customer_source,\n            inputs=["amount"],\n            function=Avg(),\n            time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=window)\n        ),\n        fe.create_feature(\n            catalog_name=CATALOG,\n            schema_name=SCHEMA,\n            source=customer_source,\n            inputs=["transaction_id"],\n            function=Count(),\n            time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=window)\n        ),\n        fe.create_feature(\n            catalog_name=CATALOG,\n            schema_name=SCHEMA,\n            source=customer_source,\n            inputs=["merchant_id"],\n            function=ApproxCountDistinct(),\n            time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=window)\n        ),\n    ])\n```\n\n### Filter Conditions\n\nUse `filter_condition` to restrict which rows are included before aggregation:\n\n```python\nhigh_value_txn_count = fe.create_feature(\n    catalog_name=CATALOG,\n    schema_name=SCHEMA,\n    source=customer_source,\n    inputs=["transaction_id"],\n    function=Count(),\n    time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=timedelta(days=30)),\n    filter_condition="amount > 100"\n)\n```\n\nUse `filter_condition` for same-level filtering (e.g., "only completed orders"). Use separate `DeltaTableSource` objects with different `entity_columns` for different aggregation levels.\n\n### Feature Naming\n\n- When you provide a `name`, use descriptive names following a consistent convention (e.g., `customer_amt_sum_7d`, `merchant_txn_count_1h`)\n- When you omit `name`, the API auto-generates names like `amount_sum_continuous_7d`\n\n## Step 4: Create Training Set\n\nUse `create_training_set` to join features with labeled data. This computes point-in-time correct feature values — each row\'s features are computed using only data available before that row\'s timestamp.\n\n```python\nlabeled_df = spark.read.table("catalog.schema.transactions")\n\ntraining_set = fe.create_training_set(\n    df=labeled_df,\n    features=features,\n    label="target_column"\n)\n\ntraining_df = training_set.load_df()\ndisplay(training_df.limit(20))\n```\n\nRequirements for the labeled DataFrame (`df`):\n\n- Must contain all `entity_columns` from feature sources\n- Must contain the `timeseries_column` from feature sources\n- Must contain the label column(s)\n- Label column names must NOT exist in the source tables defining features\n\n## Step 5: Evaluate on Holdout Data\n\nWhen evaluating on a separate holdout/test dataset, features should be computed using the full available transaction history — not just the holdout data alone. Use `DataFrameSource` to provide a union of training and test data as the feature source:\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import DataFrameSource\n\ntrain_sdf = spark.read.table("catalog.schema.train_table")\ntest_sdf = spark.read.table("catalog.schema.test_table")\nfull_history = train_sdf.unionByName(test_sdf)\n\nfull_history_source = DataFrameSource(\n    dataframe=full_history,\n    entity_columns=["customer_id"],\n    timeseries_column="transaction_time",\n    source_name="full_history"\n)\n\n# Redefine features using the full-history source instead of the original DeltaTableSource\neval_features = []\nfor window in time_windows:\n    eval_features.extend([\n        fe.create_feature(\n            catalog_name=CATALOG,\n            schema_name=SCHEMA,\n            source=full_history_source,\n            inputs=["amount"],\n            function=Sum(),\n            time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=window)\n        ),\n        # ... repeat for other features with the same definitions as training\n    ])\n\nlabeled_eval_df = spark.read.table("catalog.schema.test_table")\neval_set = fe.create_training_set(\n    df=labeled_eval_df,\n    features=eval_features,\n    label="target_column"\n)\n\neval_df = eval_set.load_df().toPandas()\n```\n\nThis ensures feature values for the holdout set reflect realistic historical context, just as they would in production.\n\n## Model Logging\n\n### Known Limitation\n\n`FeatureEngineeringClient.log_model()` does not yet support declarative feature-based training sets. Use `mlflow.sklearn.log_model` (or the appropriate MLflow flavor) directly to log the model:\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\nfrom mlflow.models import infer_signature\n\nwith mlflow.start_run(run_name="model_with_features") as run:\n    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n    signature = infer_signature(X_train, model.predict(X_train))\n    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(\n        sk_model=model,\n        artifact_path="model",\n        registered_model_name="catalog.schema.model_name",\n        signature=signature\n    )\n```\n\nAlways capture the `run` object from `mlflow.start_run()` so you can reference `run.info.run_id` later for model loading and evaluation.\n\n### When fe.log_model Becomes Supported\n\nOnce `fe.log_model` supports declarative training sets, prefer it over `mlflow.sklearn.log_model` because it records feature lineage, enabling automatic feature computation during `score_batch`:\n\n```python\n# Future: when fe.log_model supports declarative training sets\nfe.log_model(\n    model=model,\n    artifact_path="model",\n    flavor=mlflow.sklearn,\n    training_set=training_set,\n    registered_model_name="catalog.schema.model_name"\n)\n```\n\n## Common Feature Patterns\n\n### Behavioral Velocity (Fraud Detection, Anomaly Classification)\n\nDefine features that capture spending velocity and entity diversity:\n\n- **Amount aggregations**: Sum, Avg per entity across multiple windows (1h, 6h, 1d, 7d, 30d)\n- **Transaction frequency**: Count per entity across windows\n- **Entity diversity**: ApproxCountDistinct of merchants per customer, and of customers per merchant\n- **Fraud/anomaly history**: Sum of fraud labels per merchant (if available in source data)\n\n### RFM (Recency, Frequency, Monetary)\n\nApplicable to classification (churn, lead scoring) and regression (customer lifetime value):\n\n- **Recency**: Count with short windows (1d, 3d)\n- **Frequency**: Count with longer windows (30d, 90d)\n- **Monetary**: Sum of amounts with medium-to-long windows (30d, 90d)\n\n### Trend Detection\n\nUse offsets to compare recent vs. historical averages — useful for regression (forecasting) and classification (detecting behavioral shifts):\n\n```python\nfrom databricks.feature_engineering.entities import ContinuousWindow\n\nrecent_avg = fe.create_feature(\n    catalog_name=CATALOG,\n    schema_name=SCHEMA,\n    source=source,\n    inputs=["amount"],\n    function=Avg(),\n    time_window=ContinuousWindow(window_duration=timedelta(days=7))\n)\n\nhistorical_avg = fe.create_feature(\n    catalog_name=CATALOG,\n    schema_name=SCHEMA,\n    source=source,\n    inputs=["amount"],\n    function=Avg(),\n    time_window=ContinuousWindow(\n        window_duration=timedelta(days=7),\n        offset=timedelta(days=-7)\n    )\n)\n```\n\n### Static Lookups\n\nFor static reference data (property attributes, customer profiles, configuration tables), use `TableLookup` to pull attribute values as features without aggregation or windowing. This allows static features to be managed within the same declarative pipeline as time-windowed features.\n'}}]);
